# /seite new <type> "<title>"

1. Look for an existing page first: `seite search <query>`.
2. Run `seite new <type> "<title>"`. `<type>` is the collection's singular name (`post`, `doc`, `page`, …). Options: `--tags a,b`, `--draft`, `--lang xx`. It prints the path of the new file, writes the collection's schema defaults, and lists required fields to "fill in".
3. Write the body in that file and set `description:`. Fill in every field `seite new` listed. `seite templates` shows each collection's fields (type, required, allowed values); custom fields go under `extra:`, built-in ones (`title`, `description`, `tags`, …) at the top level. Change frontmatter with `seite frontmatter set <path> key=value` (custom fields are stored under `extra:` for you), or edit the YAML directly.
4. To schedule a post, give it a future `date:`; it publishes on that date (daily rebuild). `expires: YYYY-MM-DD` takes a page down after that day. `seite serve` and `seite build --future` show scheduled posts.
5. Link to other pages with relative `.md` paths, e.g. `[intro](../docs/intro.md)`.
6. Verify as in [check.md](check.md). A `frontmatter-schema` error names the field, what it expects, and the line to fix.

==> .claude/skills/seite/reference/preview.md <==
# /seite preview

1. `seite context` shows whether a dev server is already running, and its URL. If one is, use it. Never start a second server.
2. Otherwise start one in the background: `seite serve --no-repl` (add `--build` when `dist/` is missing). The default URL is `http://localhost:3000`; seite picks another port if that one is taken, and prints it.
3. Open the preview once, after the first visible change (template, CSS, a new page):
   - Claude desktop app: open the `seite` server from `.claude/launch.json` in its preview pane (it starts the server itself, so skip step 2).
   - Cursor: open the URL in Cursor's browser.
   - Anywhere else: run `seite serve --no-repl --open` instead of step 2, or give the user the URL.
4. Live reload refreshes the page after later edits. Do not reopen it.
5. Stop the server when the user is done, if you started it.

==> .claude/skills/seite/reference/theme.md <==
# /seite theme [name | description]

A theme is `templates/base.html`. Collection templates (`post.html`, `doc.html`, …) extend it and stay as they are.

## List or apply

- No argument: `seite theme list`.
- A bundled theme name: `seite theme apply <name>` (a customized `base.html` is backed up), then verify as in [check.md](check.md).

## Design a custom theme (`/seite theme <description>`)

1. Design decisions: if impeccable is installed (`.claude/skills/impeccable/` or `.agents/skills/impeccable/` exists), use `/impeccable` (Codex: `$impeccable`) for direction, type, color, and layout, and apply its result within the contract below. Otherwise decide from the description, and mention once that `seite skill install impeccable` adds design tooling.
2. Start from the closest bundled theme (`seite theme apply <name>`) and restyle it. It already has the SEO head, search, and i18n wiring.
3. Build directly in `templates/base.html` (assets in `static/`, colors from `data/brand.yaml` if it exists). Never make standalone HTML mockups to port later.
4. Preview as in [preview.md](preview.md), once after the first visible change.
5. Verify as in [check.md](check.md). If impeccable is installed, run `seite build` and then `impeccable detect dist/` once, and fix what it reports.
6. Optional: `seite theme export <name>` saves the theme as `templates/themes/<name>.tera`.

## Contract for base.html

- A full HTML document that extends nothing: `<html lang="{{ lang }}">`.
- Blocks: `title`, `head`, `extra_css`, `header`, `content` (inside `<main id="main-content">`), `footer`, `extra_js`.
- Head: everything in the `seo-requirements` context guide (canonical URL, `page.description | default(value=site.description)`, Open Graph and Twitter tags with an absolute image URL, JSON-LD, RSS/Atom links, the `/llms.txt` link, the markdown alternate inside `{% if page.markdown_url %}`, hreflang links for `translations`, `page.robots`).
- Body: `{{ page.content | safe }}` on pages; loop over `collections` on index pages; render `pagination` when it is set.
- i18n: UI text from `{{ t.<key> }}`, internal links prefixed with `{{ lang_prefix }}`, never hardcoded English.
- Search: a `role="search"` form with `<input type="search" id="search-input">` and `<div id="search-results" aria-live="polite">`. The script loads `{{ site.base_path }}{{ lang_prefix }}/search-index.json` on first input: an array of `{title, description, url, collection, tags, date, lang}`. It filters on title, description, and tags, and prefixes result URLs with `site.base_path`.
- Accessibility: a skip link to `#main-content`, visible focus styles, and `prefers-reduced-motion` respected.

==> .claude/skills/seite/reference/upgrade.md <==
# /seite upgrade

Brings this project's agent files and config up to date with the installed `seite`.

1. Run `seite upgrade --check`. It lists pending changes and exits 1 when there are some.
2. Show the list to the user. After they agree, run `seite upgrade --force`.
3. `--agents claude,codex,…` changes which coding agents the project is set up for; `--mcp` also adds the seite MCP server to their configs.
4. Verify as in [check.md](check.md).

If the installed `seite` is older than the project expects, tell the user to run `seite self-update`.

==> .codex/config.toml <==
# Codex CLI project config for this seite site.
#
# Codex only loads a project's .codex/config.toml once you trust the project:
# answer "trust" when Codex asks on first run in this directory (this records
# `[projects."<path>"] trust_level = "trusted"` in ~/.codex/config.toml).
# After that, `codex mcp list` (or /mcp inside Codex) shows the seite server.

[mcp_servers.seite]
command = "seite"
args = ["mcp"]
default_tools_approval_mode = "approve"

